WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:22.440
So a couple of years ago, I started a program to try to get the rock star tech and design

00:22.440 --> 00:27.520
people to take a year off and work in the one environment that represents pretty much

00:27.520 --> 00:34.400
everything they're supposed to hate. We have them work in government. The program is called

00:34.400 --> 00:39.720
Code for America, and it's a little bit like a Peace Corps for geeks. We select a few fellows

00:39.720 --> 00:46.100
every year, and we have them work with city governments. Instead of sending them off into

00:46.100 --> 00:50.280
the third world, we send them into the wilds of city hall, and there they make great apps.

00:50.280 --> 00:54.280
They work with city staffers, but really what they're doing is they're showing what's possible

00:54.280 --> 01:01.640
with technology today. So meet Al. Al is a fire hydrant in the city of Boston. Here it kind of

01:01.640 --> 01:05.960
looks like he's looking for a date, but what he's really looking for is for someone to shovel him

01:05.960 --> 01:09.960
out when he gets snowed in because he knows he's not very good at fighting fires when he's covered

01:09.960 --> 01:16.480
in four feet of snow. Now how did he come to be looking for help in this very unique manner? We

01:16.480 --> 01:20.240
had a team of fellows in Boston last year through the Code for America program. They were there in

01:20.240 --> 01:25.040
February, and it snowed a lot in February last year, and they noticed that the city never gets

01:25.040 --> 01:30.680
to digging out these fire hydrants. But one fellow in particular, a guy named Eric Michael Ober,

01:30.680 --> 01:35.800
noticed something else, and that's that citizens are shoveling out sidewalks right in front of

01:35.800 --> 01:41.200
these things. So he did what any good developer would do. He wrote an app. It's a cute little app

01:41.200 --> 01:46.360
where you can adopt a fire hydrant. So you agree to dig it out when it snows. If you do, you get to

01:46.360 --> 01:51.720
name it. He called the first one Al, and if you don't, someone can steal it from you. So it's got

01:51.720 --> 01:57.560
cute little game dynamics on it. So this is a modest little app. It's probably the smallest of

01:57.560 --> 02:02.360
the 21 apps that the fellows wrote last year, but it's doing something that no other government

02:02.360 --> 02:09.480
technology does. It's spreading virally. There's a guy in the IT department of the city of Honolulu

02:09.480 --> 02:15.400
who saw this app and realized that he could use it, not for snow, but to get citizens to adopt

02:15.440 --> 02:21.720
tsunami sirens. It's very important that these tsunami sirens work, but people steal the batteries

02:21.720 --> 02:26.880
out of them, so he's getting citizens to check on them. And then Seattle decided to use it to get

02:26.880 --> 02:31.960
citizens to clear out clogged storm drains, and Chicago just rolled it out to get people to sign

02:31.960 --> 02:38.320
up to shovel sidewalks when it snows. So we now know of nine cities that are planning to use this,

02:38.320 --> 02:44.400
and this is spread just frictionlessly, organically, naturally. If you know anything about government

02:44.400 --> 02:50.000
technology, you know that this isn't how it normally goes. Procuring software usually takes

02:50.000 --> 02:55.600
a couple of years. We had a team that worked on a project in Boston last year that took three

02:55.600 --> 02:59.920
people about two and a half months. It was a way that parents could figure out which were the right

02:59.920 --> 03:04.680
public schools for their kids. We were told afterwards that if that had gone through normal

03:04.680 --> 03:10.120
channels, it would have taken at least two years, and it would have cost about two million dollars.

03:10.120 --> 03:16.040
And that's nothing. There is one project in the California court system right now that so far

03:16.040 --> 03:23.000
cost taxpayers two billion dollars, and it doesn't work. And there are projects like this at every

03:23.000 --> 03:29.640
level of government. So an app that takes, you know, a couple of days to write and then spreads

03:29.640 --> 03:35.120
virally, that's sort of a shot across the bow to the institution of government. It suggests how

03:35.120 --> 03:40.440
government could work better. Not more like a private company, as many people think it should,

03:40.440 --> 03:46.320
and not even more like a tech company, but more like the internet itself. And that means

03:46.320 --> 03:55.040
permissionless, it means open, it means generative. And that's important. But what's more important

03:55.040 --> 03:59.680
about this app is that it represents how a new generation is tackling the problem of government.

03:59.680 --> 04:07.160
Not as the problem of an ossified institution, but as a problem of collective action. And that's

04:07.160 --> 04:11.920
great news, because it turns out we're very good at collective action with digital technology.

04:11.920 --> 04:17.040
Now there's a very large community of people that are building the tools that we need to do things

04:17.040 --> 04:21.920
together effectively. It's not just Code for America fellows, there are hundreds of people

04:21.920 --> 04:27.560
all over the country that are standing and writing civic apps every day in their own communities.

04:27.560 --> 04:33.960
They haven't given up on government. They are frustrated as hell with it, but they're not

04:33.960 --> 04:39.480
complaining about it, they're fixing it. And these folks know something that we've lost sight of,

04:39.480 --> 04:45.440
and that's that when you strip away all your feelings about politics and the line at the DMV

04:45.440 --> 04:52.240
and all those other things that we're really mad about, government is at its core, in the words of

04:52.240 --> 05:00.360
Tim O'Reilly, what we do together that we can't do alone. Now a lot of people have given up on

05:00.360 --> 05:06.520
government, and if you're one of those people I would ask that you reconsider, because things are

05:06.520 --> 05:14.800
changing. Politics is not changing, government is changing. And because government ultimately derives

05:14.800 --> 05:21.360
its power from us, remember we the people, how we think about it is going to affect how that change

05:21.360 --> 05:27.280
happens. Now I didn't know very much about government when I started this program, and like a lot of

05:27.280 --> 05:32.240
people I thought government was basically about getting people elected to office. Well after two

05:32.240 --> 05:39.600
years I've come to the conclusion that especially local government is about possums. This is the call

05:39.600 --> 05:44.800
center for the services and information line, it's generally where you will get if you call 311 in

05:44.800 --> 05:51.000
your city. If you should ever have the chance to staff your city's call center as our fellow Scott

05:51.040 --> 05:56.360
Silverman did as part of the program, in fact they all do that, you will find that people call

05:56.360 --> 06:02.280
government with a very wide range of issues, including having a possum stuck in your house. So Scott

06:02.280 --> 06:07.520
gets this call, he types possum into this official knowledge base, he doesn't really come up with

06:07.520 --> 06:11.760
anything, he starts with animal control, and finally he says look, you know, can you just open all the

06:11.760 --> 06:17.040
doors to your house and play music really loud and see if the thing leaves? So that worked, so

06:17.280 --> 06:23.200
boo you off for Scott. But that wasn't the end of the possums, Boston doesn't just have a call center,

06:23.200 --> 06:28.440
it has an app, a web and mobile app called Citizens Connect. Now we didn't write this app, this is the

06:28.440 --> 06:33.400
work of the very smart people in the Office of New Urban Mechanics in Boston, so one day this is an

06:33.400 --> 06:40.040
actual report, this came in, possum in my trash can, can't tell if it's dead, how do I get this removed?

06:40.040 --> 06:47.240
But what happens in Citizens Connect is different, so Scott was speaking person to person, but in

06:47.240 --> 06:52.520
Citizens Connect everything is public, so everybody can see this, and in this case a neighbor saw it,

06:52.520 --> 06:59.080
and the next report we got said, I walked over to this location, found the trash can behind the

06:59.080 --> 07:05.200
house, possum check, living, yep, turned trash can on its side, walked home, good night sweet possum.

07:05.200 --> 07:14.400
Pretty simple. So this is great, this is the digital meeting the physical, and it's also a great example

07:14.400 --> 07:19.720
of government getting in on the crowdsourcing game, but it's also a great example of government as a

07:19.720 --> 07:25.680
platform, and I don't mean necessarily a technological definition of platform here, I'm just talking about

07:25.680 --> 07:32.800
a platform for people to help themselves and to help others. So one citizen helped another citizen,

07:32.800 --> 07:38.800
but government played a key role here, it connected those two people, and it could have connected them

07:38.800 --> 07:43.360
with government services if they'd been needed, but a neighbor is a far better and cheaper

07:43.360 --> 07:49.200
alternative to government services. When one neighbor helps another, we strengthen our communities,

07:49.200 --> 07:56.520
we call animal control, it just costs a lot of money. Now one of the important things we need to

07:56.520 --> 08:02.120
think about government is that it's not the same thing as politics, and most people get that, but

08:02.120 --> 08:07.440
they think that one is the input to the other, that our input to the system of government is voting.

08:07.440 --> 08:13.240
Now how many times have we elected a political leader, and sometimes we spend a lot of energy

08:13.240 --> 08:18.680
getting a new political leader elected, and then we sit back and we expect government to reflect

08:18.680 --> 08:27.240
our values and meet our needs, and then not that much changes. That's because government is like a

08:27.360 --> 08:35.640
vast ocean, and politics is the six-inch layer on top, and what's under that is what we call bureaucracy,

08:35.640 --> 08:43.600
and we say that word with such contempt, but it's that contempt that keeps this thing that we own

08:43.600 --> 08:51.720
and we pay for as something that's working against us, this other thing, and then we're disempowering

08:51.720 --> 08:58.080
ourselves. People seem to think politics is sexy. If we want this institution to work for us,

08:58.080 --> 09:04.160
we're going to have to make bureaucracy sexy, because that's where the real work of government

09:04.160 --> 09:10.960
happens. We have to engage with the machinery of government, so that's what the Occupy SEC movement

09:10.960 --> 09:15.240
has done. Have you seen these guys? It's a group of concerned citizens that have written a very

09:15.360 --> 09:21.920
detailed 325-page report that's a response to the SEC's request for comment on the financial

09:21.920 --> 09:29.840
reform bill. That's not being politically active. That's being bureaucratically active. Now for those

09:29.840 --> 09:35.000
of us who've given up on government, it's time that we asked ourselves about the world that we

09:35.000 --> 09:40.240
want to leave for our children. You have to see the enormous challenges that they're going to

09:40.240 --> 09:46.840
face. Do we really think we're going to get where we need to go without fixing the one institution

09:46.840 --> 09:53.520
that can act on behalf of all of us? We can't do without government, but we do need it to be more

09:53.520 --> 09:59.200
effective. The good news is that technology is making it possible to fundamentally reframe the

09:59.200 --> 10:06.360
function of government in a way that can actually scale by strengthening civil society, and there's

10:06.360 --> 10:10.760
a generation out there that's grown up on the internet, and they know that it's not that hard

10:10.760 --> 10:18.280
to do things together. You just have to architect the systems the right way. Now the average age of

10:18.280 --> 10:25.560
our fellows is 28, so I am begrudgingly almost a generation older than most of them. This is a

10:25.560 --> 10:30.920
generation that's grown up taking their voices pretty much for granted. They're not fighting

10:30.920 --> 10:36.520
that battle that we're all fighting about who gets to speak. They all get to speak. They can

10:36.520 --> 10:43.240
express their opinion on any channel at any time, and they do. So when they're faced with the problem

10:43.240 --> 10:51.480
of government, they don't care as much about using their voices. They're using their hands. They're

10:51.480 --> 10:56.760
using their hands to write applications that make government work better, and those applications let

10:56.840 --> 11:04.200
us use our hands to make our communities better. That could be shoveling out a hydrant, pulling a

11:04.200 --> 11:10.920
weed, turning over a garbage can with a possum in it, and certainly we could have been shoveling out

11:10.920 --> 11:17.000
those fire hydrants all along, and many people do, but these apps are like little digital reminders

11:17.000 --> 11:22.280
that we're not just consumers, and we're not just consumers of government putting in our taxes and

11:22.280 --> 11:30.520
getting back services. We're more than that. We're citizens, and we're not going to fix government

11:30.520 --> 11:39.000
until we fix citizenship. So the question I have for all of you here, when it comes to the big

11:39.000 --> 11:45.960
important things that we need to do together, all of us together, are we just going to be a crowd of

11:45.960 --> 11:53.640
voices, or are we also going to be a crowd of hands? Thank you.

